# tools/moap_coherence_gate.mode to de-fang a scanner false-positive without wedging publish.
# R15-proven both ways in tests/tools/test_moap_coherence_gate.py.
python3 tools/moap_coherence_gate.py || exit 1

# ARCHIVE-QUESTION GATE (2026-07-28, DIRECTOR_RULING_NO_QUESTION_LEFT_UNANSWERED §2): a commit that
# archives a [DIRECTOR-RULING]/[STEER] into docs/staging/done/ while it still carries an UNANSWERED
# question (status open/carried in docs/observability/open_question_register.json) is STRUCTURALLY
# REFUSED. "A ruling is not fully consumed while any of its questions is unanswered" — enforced
# mechanically, not by discipline. Fail-closed: an unreadable archived-ruling blob or a corrupt
# register refuses the archive (never silently unblocks). Reuses open_question_register's blocking
# predicate (no re-implementation). R15-proven both ways in tests/tools/test_ruling_archive_question_gate.py.
python3 tools/ruling_archive_question_gate.py || exit 1

# CONSOLIDATION RHYTHM (AO6, 2026-08-08, DIRECTOR_PROGRAMME_ARCHITECTED_OUT §RHYTHM): "every epoch
# close includes a consolidation pass -- duplicates found, orphans wired or retired". The atom's own
# origin_note rules out the obvious implementation: "an epoch-close consolidation pass that lives
# only as prose in a checklist WILL evaporate exactly as the earlier exhortations did." So the duty
# is a GATE. An epoch is CLOSED when every one of its maturity-map atoms has reached its target --
# read from the map, never declared -- and a commit that moves an epoch from open to closed without
# a COMMITTED pass record in docs/observability/consolidation_ledger.jsonl is REFUSED. Fires only on
# that transition (a map edit closing nothing pays nothing), and only when the map is staged.
# R12: it checks that the pass HAPPENED and never scores how much was pruned -- a pass that
# deliberately keeps every orphan, with reasons, is rc 0, and no orphan count can turn this red.
# R15-proven both ways in tests/tools/test_consolidation_rhythm.py (9 source mutations).
python3 tools/consolidation_rhythm.py --gate || exit 1

# SIZE + CLONE RATCHET (SP3, 2026-08-03, DIRECTOR_RULING_SHARED_PRIMITIVES_AND_CODE_STANDARDS §3):
# a monotonic ceiling on module size and cross-file structural duplication, so structural debt
# drains as a SIDE-EFFECT of ordinary work and never needs a remediation sprint. Four rules: no
# baselined file exceeds its own frozen count; a file this commit TOUCHES comes out no larger than
# it went in (the rule that actually drains); new files/functions are capped (600/60, NEW only --
# the 43 existing oversized files are grandfathered, never retroactively broken); the clone count
# never exceeds its frozen ceiling. Anti-Goodhart (ruling §7): these are tripwires and reported
# facts, NEVER scores to minimise -- there is no lower bound and there must never be one.
# Currently rollout_state=warn (ruling §8): it PRINTS and logs to
# docs/observability/size_ratchet_warnings.jsonl but does NOT block. The warn->gate flip is a
# deliberate one-line diff to docs/observability/size_ratchet_baseline.json after one retro
# cadence -- never an auto-flip (that would be the silent-state-change class R2/R7 forbid).
# Staging-aware (an out-of-scope commit pays nothing) and fail-closed in BOTH states: a missing/
# corrupt baseline or an unparseable source REFUSES, because an unavailable check is a failed
# check. No bypass flag by design -- exceptions go through tools/size_ratchet_override.py, which
# logs a named decision scoped to one file at one line count. R15-proven both ways in
# tests/tools/test_size_ratchet_gate.py (13 source mutations, each firing its own named test).
python3 tools/size_ratchet_gate.py || exit 1

# THE ORPHAN RATCHET (2026-08-12, director instruction; brief =
# docs/staging/done/WORKER_REPORT_NO_CALLER_CLASS_CENSUS_2026-08-09.md -- 13 instances of the
# no-caller class in 13 days, EIGHT of them found by accident weeks later).
#
# A commit may not ADD a module that nothing runs. The standing ~400 are frozen in
# docs/design/orphan_baseline.json and nobody has to fix them to commit; what fails is GROWTH,
# which is the difference between a report everyone ignores and a control. Entrypoints come from
# the COMMITTED SCHEDULE (systemd units, timers, hooks), never from `if __name__ == "__main__"` --
# a CLI nothing schedules is an orphan, and that blind spot is census instance #10 exactly.
#
# Fail-closed both ways: a broken schedule parser (vacuity) and a truncated index walk (coverage,
# oracled against git rather than the same filesystem walk) both REFUSE rather than certify, since
# an under-reporting orphan check AUTHORISES the thing it exists to prevent. To record a
# deliberately dormant module, run `python3 tools/orphan_ratchet.py --freeze` in the SAME commit
# so the decision is on the record. R15-proven both ways in tests/tools/test_orphan_ratchet.py.
python3 tools/orphan_ratchet.py || exit 1

# THE COMPANY HAS NO ROUTE TO A REAL ENDPOINT (director ruling, 2026-08-18): "the supplier
# doesn't seek approval -- it's refused by construction. If company-side code has to ask,
# someone eventually says yes, and the breach arrives as a reasonable decision." The rule is
# a CAPABILITY, not a host list, because a host list would have to be relaxed at go-live --
