# that pin the spine as NOT a subject of the control.
python3 -m tools.half_hourly_dependency_ratchet || exit 1

# ── GATE 14: a running total may only be read in the order it was accumulated ────────────────
# THE CLASS FIX (R10) for the treasury-drawdown artefact found 2026-08-24. `run_phase2b`
# settles one customer's whole term before starting the next, so `treasury_cash_balance_gbp`
# -- a PORTFOLIO running total stamped on each record as it is produced -- means something in
# accumulation order and nothing in any other. `annual_report` sorted the book by
# (settlement_date, settlement_period) before walking it into `_drawdown_events`, interleaving
# balances produced at completely different points in the term loop. Measured on a real
# end-2017 run: 0 genuine drawdowns published as 6,747, each a penny apart, with a RAG rating
# computed from them. The number was not too high -- the phenomenon did not happen.
#
# The finding closed its own INSTANCE with a register that folds the drawdown during the run.
# It left the class open in writing: nothing checked that a balance stamped in accumulation
# order is only ever read in accumulation order, and three other running totals ride the same
# records. This gate is that check, and it found TWO MORE the finding did not know about --
# both `treasury_end`, a published balance-sheet figure, one of them (segment_report) with no
# register fallback at all.
#
# RATCHET, not a freeze: the three reads that exist today are frozen WITH THEIR COUNTS, so a
# NEW read fails, a SECOND read of an already-frozen (module, shape, field) fails rather than
# hiding inside the baseline, and a repaired entry left behind fails too. Every frozen entry
# names its repair. Those repairs MOVE PUBLISHED FIGURES and so land measured (R14), which is
# why this gate ignores them while the default mode -- `python3 -m tools.running_total_order`
# -- reports them as the standing red.
# R15-proven both ways in tests/tools/test_running_total_order.py (15 tests, synthetic trees
# with hand-written answers), including the null controls that keep it usable (an
# order-preserving `yr[-1][field]` and a re-sorted read of a NON-running-total field are both
# silent) and the fail-closed path: a scan that sees no running-total field must RAISE, never
# report a clean tree it never looked at.
python3 -m tools.running_total_order --gate || exit 1

# ── GATE 15: an atom that CLAIMS A LEVEL must name a file_scope that exists ──────────────────
# THIRTEEN atoms were doing otherwise when this landed (2026-08-24), eleven of them broken by a
# single commit -- 03dd8c49e, "The five tabs are the site now", which deleted eleven pages on a
# director ruling. `E2_revenue_reconciliation` was the worst: L3 claimed, all three of its named
# paths gone, and its own control red for four days as a result. A level is a claim about
# evidence; a deleted path is not evidence, and nothing anywhere said so.
#
# That commit's message names the class it was fixing on the OTHER side of the same boundary --
# "a generator that outlives its page is how a deleted surface returns" -- and rewrote 87
# controls carrying literal page lists to derive from the built site. Nothing looked at the MAP.
# An atom that outlives its files is the same defect wearing governance clothes, and it is worse,
# because the map is what the draw reads to decide what to work on.
#
# KEYS ON THE CLAIM, NOT ON THE PATH. A `level_current: 0` proposal naming the files it WOULD
# create is correct and there are 17 of them; refusing those would make the map unable to
# describe future work. NO FROZEN BASELINE: the thirteen were repaired in the arming commit, so
# this starts at zero with nothing to erode.
# TWO KINDS OF NOTHING. On its first run inside this hook it found one the map-side scan had
# missed entirely: `PB2_opening_book_won_not_assigned` at L3 naming a build record that was on
# disk and had NEVER BEEN COMMITTED. The extract is built from git objects, so the gate sees what
# a fresh clone would -- which is the whole point. DELETED and NOT IN GIT are reported apart,
# because "missing" sends a reader hunting a deletion that never happened.
# R15-proven in tests/tools/test_scope_evidence_ratchet.py (14 tests), including the parser
# cross-check that killed the first implementation -- a regex reader silently produced an EMPTY
# file_scope for three atoms, and an empty scope means "nothing to check" -- and the guard that a
# git probe which cannot run degrades to existence-only instead of inventing an uncommitted find.
python3 -m tools.scope_evidence_ratchet || exit 1
